<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">County Palatine of Pembroke</h1><p class="st-meta">1138 CE – 1536 CE (approx.) · capital Pembroke</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Norman marcher lordship in south-west Wales, base of Strongbow's invasion of Ireland and birthplace of Henry VII.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnulf-de-montgomery">Arnulf de Montgomery</a> built the first castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembroke-pembrokeshire">Pembroke</a> in 1093, and it held out against Welsh counter-attacks. In 1138 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-king-of-england">King Stephen</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-de-clare-1st-earl-of-pembroke">Gilbert de Clare</a> Earl of Pembroke, giving the lordship palatine powers. English and Flemish settlers occupied south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a>, creating the 'Little <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>'.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The earls exercised royal-style jurisdiction within the county. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert's</a> son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Richard de Clare</a>, Strongbow, led the invasion of Ireland in 1170 from here. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-marshal-1st-earl-of-pembroke">William Marshal</a>, who married Strongbow's heiress, rebuilt <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembroke-castle">Pembroke Castle</a> with its great round keep. Later holders included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valence">Valence</a> and Hastings families and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jasper-tudor">Jasper Tudor</a>, whose nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-vii-of-england">Henry VII</a> was born in the castle in 1457.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laws-in-wales-acts-1535-and-1542">Laws in Wales Act</a> of 1536 ended its special status, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembroke-pembrokeshire">Pembroke</a> became the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pembrokeshire">Pembrokeshire</a>. The earldom title continued in the Herbert family.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/county-palatine-of-pembroke.jpg" alt="County Palatine of Pembroke" width="320" height="249"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coronet_of_a_British_Earl.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1138 CE – 1536 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pembroke</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gilbert de Clare, William Marshal, Jasper Tudor</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Anglo-Norman French, Middle English, Welsh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Earldom of Pembroke, Lordship of Pembroke</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1337&amp;focus=county-palatine-of-pembroke" aria-label="County Palatine of Pembroke on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4569 390 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where County Palatine of Pembroke lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4889 646L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4889 646h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4889 646h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pembroke. <a href="/atlas?year=1337&amp;focus=county-palatine-of-pembroke">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
